#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Prints one token and exits 0. It exists to be RUN, by git, so that something
# can witness that git runs this repository's hooks at all.
#
# Not a native hook name, and deliberately so: git never invokes this by itself,
# only `git hook run --allow-unknown-hook-name gqlc-liveness-probe` does, so
# adding it changes the behaviour of no commit, push, merge or checkout. What it
# adds is a hook git will resolve through core.hooksPath exactly the way it
# resolves pre-commit — same lookup, same executable test, same relative-path
# rules — with none of the cost or side effects of running a real gate.
#
# .githooks/verify-hooks-live is the reader. The token is spelled there too and
# the two have to agree; that is a fact spelled twice. What held them together
# was .githooks/tests/verify-hooks-live-test.sh, which failed if a run of the
# real verifier against a fixture carrying the real probe did not come back
# live. PR #1595 deleted it and nothing replaced it, so the two spellings are
# now held in agreement by nothing: change the token in one file and the
# verifier reports every hook dead (bd gqlc-u2nim).
#
# It writes to stdout rather than to a file. `git hook run` redirects a hook's
# stdout to ITS stderr, so the reader captures both streams and looks for the
# token anywhere in them; a witness file would need a writable location chosen
# without knowing whose repository this is, and /tmp here is a shared tmpfs that
# has run out of inodes (bd gqlc-vze6).
printf '%s\n' 'gqlc-hooks-live-witness'
